            <div xml:id="d368">

                <pb facs="0469" n="374" sameAs="frus:frus1936v04/pg374"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/8335: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Counselor of Embassy in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Peck</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-11-11T12:00:00+08:00">November 11,
                        1936—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received November 11—10:15 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>326. My 325, November 10, 1 p.m. Japanese Embassy has informed news
                    representatives that the interview yesterday dealt with proposals as a whole and
                    although no specific agreement was reached the viewpoints of the two parties
                    were perceptibly closer. Suma has informed one American press representative
                    since his return from Japan that he found the Chinese leaders had passed through
                    their mood of self-confidence and uncompromising rejection of Japanese proposals
                    and were again taking the proposals seriously. Suma thought this change might
                    have been occasioned by his warning to them not as an unlooked for but as a
                    plain fact that if these negotiations terminated through futility an incident
                    might occur which could not be handled diplomatically.</p>
                <p>Sent to Peiping.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Peck</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
